  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nThe upcoming weekend calls for the Pacific ridge to remain dominant \nacross our region, but it will be in a weakening phase and is likely \nto get knocked down by a fast moving disturbance passing across the \nFour Corners area. Even with H5 heights dropping to between 580-\n583dm, temperatures are not expected to drop much with lower desert \nhighs at least maintaining mid 70s for highs. The weather pattern \nsuggests another brief shortwave ridge moving through on Monday \nand/or Tuesday, but models are favoring a gradual increase in \nmoisture early next week pushing PWAT anomalies to around 150-175% \nof normal by Tuesday. Temperatures are not expected to change much \nearly next week despite the expected increase in clouds. Models then \nfavor a more significant Pacific disturbance approaching the \nCalifornia coast and then likely passing through our region during \nthe middle of next week. The timing, strength, and exact track of \nthe system is still quite uncertain, but there are at least decent \nsigns it will contain adequate moisture for some precipitation \nchances for our region. We expect it will be at least a few more \ndays until we have a good idea on rain potential for next week's \nweather system.",
